old.text:
A node's status is draft or approved. That of a section and of a domain's header is built from the nodes under it (§s-9kfdycuq34beqvs). What follows holds of every other node.

Approved means that one of the node's owners (§s-67vekyup7y8smap) approved its present wording: its title, text and reason, and the values its kind adds beside them, such as the fields of a RecordType. Otherwise the node is a draft.

Any change to that wording, an owner's own included, makes the node a draft until one of its owners approves the wording it then holds. So does a withdrawal of its approval, which names whoever made it. Anyone may withdraw the approval of a node outside the SpecSheriff guidelines a project takes (§s-bsya3febm6u64ab), but that of the rule that approves the specification (§s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2).

The status stays as it is through a change to:

- the node's links or its metadata;
- who its owners are;
- what is built into it from other nodes, such as the values gathered beside a field (§s-ju5vcanbjph7mgh);
- where it stands.

new.text:
A node's status is `draft` or `approved`. The status of a section and of a domain's header is built from the nodes under it (§s-9kfdycuq34beqvs). What follows holds of every other node.

A node is approved when one of its owners (§s-67vekyup7y8smap) approved its present wording. That wording is its title, text and reason, and the values its NodeKind adds beside them, such as `RecordType.fields`. Otherwise the node is a draft.

Any change to that wording, an owner's own included, makes the node a draft until one of its owners approves the wording it then holds. So does a withdrawal of its approval. A withdrawal names whoever made it.

Anyone may withdraw the approval of a node, except that of:

- a node of the SpecSheriff guidelines a project takes (§s-bsya3febm6u64ab);
- the rule that approves the specification (§s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2).

The status stays as it is through a change to:

- the node's links or its metadata;
- who its owners are;
- what is built into it from other nodes, such as the values gathered beside a field (§s-ju5vcanbjph7mgh);
- where it stands.

Of a user interface, the base specification states what a user can rely on in any instance:

- what is shown;
- what it means;
- in what order;
- in which states;
- what the user can do.

How it looks and is built is described in the technical appendix: placement, size, colour, icon, the sort of control, where a state is kept.

The technical appendix describes how this repository builds the system: its stack, how each domain is built and how the user interface looks. It describes them in the detail an instance needs to repeat them. Every part but the appendix and the guidelines is the base specification.

The appendix is no domain and stands at no level. It stands beside the domains, as contracts does. It holds:

- a section for each domain it implements, under that domain's name;
- a section for the stack;
- while any problem is set aside, a section for its technical debt (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw).

The appendix never contradicts a rule of the base specification but extends and refines it. A rule too narrow for an implementation is widened in the base, and its detail goes to the technical appendix.

A node of the appendix describes how the nodes of the base specification it implements are to be carried out. It does so in enough detail to be read only one way: exact values, examples and the edge cases of that implementation. It never restates what they state.

Each node of the appendix implements at least one node of the base specification, except:

- a section, which requires nothing (§s-7445ahgjck2aefg);
- a choice covering the whole system, which stands in the section of the stack;
- the technical debt (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw).

An instance changes the technical appendix for itself. What instances vary within it is a setting, described with its default.

The section of the technical debt holds rules alone. Each of them describes one problem of the default implementation as it stands. Each refines the nodes the default implementation falls short of there, one of the base specification at least. Where the problem lies in a technology the default implementation uses, its rule names the version of that technology it was seen in.

Standing there is the whole of the choice to set the problem aside:

- its rule carries no reason;
- no decision records that choice;
- the change that solves the problem removes the rule.

The guidelines are divided as the specification is, into their base and the appendix of their tools.

The base of the guidelines states what is held, what is checked, when, and what a failure costs. It names no technology.

The appendix of the tools describes how one toolset carries that base out:

- its commands;
- its hooks;
- where it keeps the files;
- how it reads code;
- who runs its reviewer.

Each rule of that appendix implements at least one node of the base.

The appendix of the tools is not the technical appendix. The technical appendix describes how the system is built, never the tools of the specification.

The nodes of a project's own NodeKind, declared in the extension of the schema (§s-eferktypcj7njt5), are held as rules are in these respects:

- they are numbered as rules are (§s-kag2m9tzdnduxdx);
- they count as rules of the section they stand in (§s-7445ahgjck2aefg);
- unless the extension declares that they need no code, they are implemented and tested as a rule is (§s-6fw72j65jkqrfpf, §s-rah8b37kytb682y).

A declaration that they need no code exempts them from being implemented (§s-6fw72j65jkqrfpf).

A rule of the project's own guidelines, refining this one, lists each such NodeKind with whether its nodes need code. Its `TextRule.list` names the extension as its declaration, in `declaredIn`. The check of lists compares the list with that declaration (§s-jau82aq9juv9zzh).

A structure of data the domains pass to one another or keep in a store is described by a node of its own, a RecordType. The RecordType states:

- its name;
- what it is;
- each of its fields, with the field's type in neutral words, any bounds on the field's value included, whether the field is required and what it says;
- what happens to a field nobody declared;
- where another RecordType refers to it by an identifier, the field that identifies it.

The rules that bind one field to another are rules linked to the RecordType.

The implementation's types and RuntimeChecks are generated from the RecordTypes and never written beside them. Which mechanism performs the RuntimeChecks is described in the technical appendix.

Where a RecordType's fields depend on the values of some of its own fields, the RecordType names those fields as choosing its variant.

A value that brings fields of its own has a RecordType variant. That variant is a RecordType in the part of the specification declaring the value (§s-xfwuvd3funutmsw). It:

- refines the RecordType;
- names the values it answers to;
- declares the fields those values bring.

A value that brings none has no variant. A record holding such a value holds only its RecordType's own fields.

A domain's specification opens with its header. The header is a node stating:

- what the domain does;
- its owners, where it names them (§s-67vekyup7y8smap);
- the level it stands at;
- where its code is.

The header's `consumes` links name the sections of other domains the domain uses.

The header also carries the index of the domain's sections, built from the sections and never written by hand. For each section the index holds:

- its sid, its title and its text, which states what the section is responsible for;
- the domains that use it.

new.reason:
Whoever works on one domain loads its header and learns from it alone, without loading the rest:

- what the domain is responsible for;
- who answers for its wording;
- how it is divided;
- what it takes from the others.

The index is built rather than written, because a table of contents kept by hand is a second copy of the sections and drifts from them.

new.text:
The domains are ordered by the `consumes` links of their headers:

- a domain stands after every domain it uses;
- it stands as close as possible before the domains that use it;
- where the links put two domains in the same place, they stand in the order of their names.

The domains do not use one another in a cycle.

A node declares the links it makes, each naming the node it points at. For every other node it cites, it declares at least one link naming that node. A citation is `§` followed by the cited node's sid. It counts in any of the node's properties, text quoted as code included, except these:
- its links;
- the nodes under it;
- a header's index of its sections;
- the values gathered beside a field.

The links pointing at the node stand in the same list. Each names the node it comes from and is read from this node's end. They are rebuilt from the links the nodes declare: every such entry is removed first and built again. They are never written by hand.

A node of the archive is held to neither: it holds no link pointing at it, and none it declares is built into another node.

A node of the SpecSheriff guidelines holds only the links pointing at it from the SpecSheriff guidelines.

old.text:
A rule is bound to its implementation by three records:

- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, on the symbol in the code that implements it;
- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, declared by the test written from it where a run of the tests reports it;
- in the node's metadata, the lists of that code and those tests.

All three are written by hand, and they must agree.

new.text:
A rule is bound to its implementation by three records:

- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, on the symbol in the code that implements it;
- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, declared by the test written from it where a run of the tests reports it;
- the node's `meta.code` and `meta.tests`, the lists of that code and those tests.

All three are written by hand. They must agree.

Every symbol of the system's code (§s-hheus6f395ynqvc) outside its tests — a function, a class or a member of one, a type, a constant, a component — carries a SpecTag naming the node it implements. This holds wherever in the code the symbol stands and whichever domain the node belongs to. A symbol inside another is covered by the SpecTag of the one it stands in.

Some symbols implement none of the system's logic but are required by the technology the system is built with. Such a symbol carries instead a SpecTag that names no node and gives its reason. Examples are a module's re-exports and the glue a framework, a library or the infrastructure needs.

Only a symbol whose code a commit changes must name an approved node. A change to its SpecTags is a change to its code. These change no code:
- rewording the rest of its comments;
- moving a citation onto another sid;
- renaming an identifier everywhere it is spelled.

Every approved rule is implemented, and so is every approved node of a project's own NodeKind that needs code (§s-vsh384ybt4w25a2). Such a rule or node is implemented when both hold:
- a symbol of the system's code carries its SpecTag;
- a test carries its SpecTag and passes.

A SpecTag or a test naming a rule that implements such a rule or node counts for it. The implementing rule may implement it directly or through rules that implement those in turn.

The requirement to be implemented holds for every rule of the specification except those of:
- the SpecSheriff guidelines;
- the project's own guidelines;
- the technical debt (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw).

A node of the project's own guidelines exempts a rule, or a node of a project's own NodeKind, from being implemented. It does so through an `exempts` link to that rule or node, or to a section it stands under (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm). The exempting node gives the reason in its own text.

The map is the specification seen from above. It is a mindmap drawn from the nodes each time it is built. It holds no fact of its own.

The map shows the structure and the status of each node, and nothing else. The structure is a domain, the parts under it, then the rules, down to the individual rule. It is drawn from the nesting, the titles and the level of each domain alone.

A branch opens and closes, so a reader goes to the part they came for while the whole stays in view around it.

The branches stand in this order:
- the guidelines first;
- then contracts;
- then a branch for each level of the domains, from the top, holding its domains in the order §s-uw9n84edf7cbe9n gives;
- the technical appendix last.

A symbol of the system's code outside its tests, or a test, that carries no SpecTag is reported rather than refused where all of these hold:
- the project's specification is a draft;
- the commit does not change the symbol or the test.

A project's own guidelines may add checks. They are listed in a rule of theirs refining this one, with what each checks and what a failure costs. Each counts among the checks of §s-jau82aq9juv9zzh:
- it is held to all §s-jau82aq9juv9zzh says of a check;
- it runs wherever those checks run;
- it refuses unless the rule listing it says it reports.

The independent reviewer of a commit is given the following, and nothing of the session that wrote the code:
- the commit's change, less the files generated from the specification;
- the text and the reason of the nodes the commit's message and the SpecTags on its symbols outside the tests name;
- for such a node that is a RecordType, also `RecordType.fields`;
- for such a node of a project's own NodeKind, also the properties its NodeKind declares (§s-vsh384ybt4w25a2).

Each file left out is named to the reviewer as holding one of these, which the check of the generated copies compares with what the nodes produce:
- the types and RuntimeChecks generated from the RecordTypes;
- what a project's generator wrote from the nodes it names (§s-km4x4s26kv7pjze).

The reviewer refuses the commit where the code does something no named node's text states, or leaves out what one requires. It counts as carried out:
- what a RecordType requires, by the types and RuntimeChecks generated from it (§s-e877bnfa559tvk9);
- what the rule listing a project's generator says it writes, by what that generator wrote.

The reviewer names the node and the line of each finding.

A review that cannot be held — no reviewer to run, a run that does not end, an answer that cannot be read — refuses the commit and says why.

Where an agent writes the specification, the tests or the code, the checks of the specification and of the code run after each of its edits. What they refuse is reported to the agent.

Before an edit is made, it is refused where it would do any of these:
- leave a node approved over a wording for which no approval stands (§s-5ah25f6gy99panq), a section and a domain's header left out;
- change the code written for a rule, or for a node of a project's own NodeKind that needs code (§s-vsh384ybt4w25a2), that no test carries the SpecTag of (§s-rah8b37kytb682y);
- change the code of a project's own check or generator while no test carries the SpecTag of the rule listing it (§s-sfjka4zc4rhfu67);
- change the approvals otherwise than through the procedure that records an approval or withdraws one.

Where a rule restricts something by a list, it says whether the list names what is allowed or what is forbidden. It chooses by which mistake would go unnoticed:
- where something left out would go unnoticed, the list names what is forbidden, as the records a backup leaves out;
- where something let in would go unnoticed, the list names what is allowed, as the fields a public API returns.

The parts of the specification are kept under `specs/`:

- each domain under `specs/<domain>/`, opening with its header in `specs/<domain>/00-<domain>.json`;
- contracts under `specs/contracts/`;
- the technical appendix under `specs/tech/`, each of its sections in a file of its own — a domain's in `<domain>.json`, the stack's in `stack.json`, the technical debt's in `debt.json`;
- the guidelines under `specs/_guidelines/`.

A part divided into one file per section keeps its files in its directory.

In `specs/_guidelines/`, a project's own guidelines are kept in `project.json`. In the repository the SpecSheriff guidelines are written in, the base is kept there in `spec.json` and the appendix of the tools in `tools.json`. Every other project reads the base and the appendix of the tools from the plugin of its agent (§s-zh2vcr3xjumppg5, §s-2pdy9gw2jrjvfb4).

The schema is kept in `specs/_schema/` of the repository the SpecSheriff guidelines are written in. Every other project reads it from the plugin. The schema is made of these files:

- `node.schema.json` for a node and its metadata;
- `section-file.schema.json` for the file of a part;
- `extension.schema.json`, the schema of an extension.

A project's extension of the schema (§s-eferktypcj7njt5) is kept in `specs/_schema/project.schema.json`. It is validated against the schema of an extension and read together with the schema (§s-xzcw3hv4hmxz7h7). Any other file another project keeps under `specs/_schema/` is refused as a copy of the schema.

Approvals are kept in `specs/_approvals.jsonl`. The archive of removed nodes is kept in `specs/_archive/<name>.json`.

`specs/_viewer/` (the map) and `md-version/` (the readable export) are rebuilt after every change to the specification. Neither is stored in git.

The SpecSheriff tools provide the command `sheriff`. In a terminal it is run as `node <tools>/dist/cli.js`, from the tools a plugin keeps on the machine (§s-4mej6ccn7xu3d3p). `<tools>` is the data directory of the plugin for Claude Code, or the `tools/` of the plugin for Codex CLI in Codex's cache.

Their plugin for pytest, the package `pytest-specsheriff`, is installed by pip from the branch `release`: `pip install "pytest-specsheriff @ git+https://github.com/non4me/SpecSheriff@release#subdirectory=skills/pytest-specsheriff"`.

The settings of the SpecSheriff tools that a project shares are kept in `sheriff.config.json` at the root of the repository. The settings of the reviewer's runs, which each user confirms, are kept in the user's git configuration (§s-xr5xxa9cxf2mq5u). The setting `"writesSpecSheriffGuidelines": true` marks the repository the SpecSheriff guidelines are written in.

Among the settings, glob patterns name where the system's code lives and where its tests live. The code and the tests are read for SpecTags, and a file named neither as code nor as tests is read for neither. That is how the repository's configuration, its own tools and their tests are kept out of the system's code by where they live (§s-hheus6f395ynqvc).

Among the settings, too, is the command that runs each check and each generator the project's own guidelines add (§s-2fau85dfyyeq66m, §s-km4x4s26kv7pjze). For each of them, the settings also hold the glob patterns naming the files of its code and of its tests (§s-utf4mbt7ref8g9c).

The documents are the markdown files the repository keeps outside `specs/`.

The tools of the specification are the files of the SpecSheriff tools. A project keeps only the files `sheriff init` writes. The repository the SpecSheriff guidelines are written in keeps them under `skills/`, where the tests are read for the SpecTags they declare.

The following are found in `project.json`:

- the specification's owners are those its top node names;
- the levels the domains stand at are the members of the `TextRule.list` carried by its rule that refines §s-nkyty4dy9x2v2a4;
- the rule that approves the specification is its rule that refines §s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2;
- the files kept as fixtures of tests are those named by the glob patterns its rule refining §s-6hha5en226aj8hu lists.

`sheriff validate`, `sheriff binding`, `sheriff citations`, `sheriff guard`, `sheriff message` and `sheriff review` implement the checks as the rules of §s-4kd2pave7vm4hmd describe them.

`sheriff init` sets a repository up for the SpecSheriff tools (§s-7unxytshhc4md54). `sheriff remove` takes SpecSheriff out of it (§s-7unxytshhc4md54).

`sheriff reindex` is run after every change to the specification. It does the following:

- it gives a sid to every node that has none;
- it computes every node's location from where it is;
- it empties every incoming list and rebuilds it from the outgoing ones;
- it rebuilds the index in every domain's header from the domain's sections;
- beside each field of type `one of`, it writes the values gathered for it from elsewhere in the specification, each with the sid of the node it comes from (§s-ju5vcanbjph7mgh);
- it makes a draft each approved node whose latest line for it and its wording's fingerprint is not an approval. It leaves out of this a section, a domain's header and the SpecSheriff guidelines the project takes.

It then writes the status of each section and domain's header from the nodes under it (§s-9kfdycuq34beqvs).

`sheriff reindex` adds the rule `sheriff init` writes, as a draft, at every run where `project.json` holds no rule refining §s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2 (§s-7unxytshhc4md54).

Before anything else, `sheriff reindex` renames each key and value an earlier release of the SpecSheriff tools wrote under another name. In every file of the specification and of its archive, it renames:

- the key `id` to `location`;
- the key `rationale` to `reason`;
- the NodeKinds `record` and `recordType` to `RecordType`;
- the NodeKind `rule` to `TextRule`;
- the NodeKind `domain` to `header`;
- a node's status `""` to `draft`;
- a node's status `checked` to `approved`;
- the key `owner` of a domain's header or a section, holding an email, to `owners`, holding a list holding that email.

A renamed key keeps its place among the node's keys. `sheriff reindex` renames those NodeKinds also where a field's values are the sids of a NodeKind's nodes, and where the project's extension of the schema adds properties to a NodeKind.

A field of type `one of` whose own list an earlier release wrote empty takes the values the domains add as its source instead. Under that release, the empty list stood for those values.

`sheriff reindex` also renames `specs/_confirmations.jsonl` to `specs/_approvals.jsonl`. In each line of that file, it renames the key `owner` to `by`. Where `specs/_approvals.jsonl` already stands, it appends those lines to it and removes the old file.

Then, in every file it rewrites, `sheriff reindex` merges the values an object holds under one key, each into those written before it:

- two lists into one, holding the members of the first and then those of the second that the first does not hold;
- two objects key by key in the same way, each key where it was first written;
- two values JSON Schema holds equal into one.

It names every key it merged, with the file and each value's line. It refuses any other two values under one key, naming the file, the key and each one's line and value. It then writes no file.

`sheriff reindex` also rewrites each test that names its node by a bare sid, the form an earlier release of the SpecSheriff tools read. A bare sid is a word of the form of a sid that stands in one of these places:

- it opens the test's name, followed by a space or the name's end;
- it stands alone as the argument of the test's mark.

In the files the settings name as the tests of the system's code or of a project's own check or generator, `sheriff reindex` puts `@spec ` before that sid. Each node's `meta.tests` then names the test by its new name. `sheriff reindex` names each file it rewrites.

`sheriff approve` records an approval or its withdrawal (§s-v4qus7raqnwzneb).

`sheriff export` writes the readable export in markdown, one file for each file of the specification, each node as §s-k4nqjr96ux7a5m7 says.

`sheriff render` writes the map, the mindmap of the specification's structure drawn from its nodes (§s-rpv4fkdqqguptqd).

`sheriff guide` prints the following:

- every node of the guidelines — the base, the appendix of the tools and the project's own — as §s-k4nqjr96ux7a5m7 says;
- the figures counted off the model as it prints: how many nodes each part holds and how many of them are approved;
- whether the specification is approved.

`sheriff show` prints a node, found by its sid or its location, as §s-k4nqjr96ux7a5m7 says.

`sheriff model` prints as JSON the model of the specification where it runs. The model holds every node as its file holds it, with its location, the part and the file it stands in, and the owners named nearest above it. A project's own check or generator is handed the model on its standard input (§s-2fau85dfyyeq66m, §s-km4x4s26kv7pjze).

`sheriff todos` prints, as text, every item of `meta.todos` in the specification where it runs, the archive left out, and the SpecSheriff guidelines too where the project takes them. The items come the newest first, and those of one day in the order the specification reads. Each item comes with its date, who its author is, what is to be done, and the sid, location and title of its node (§s-t28j22vbdmmeqnh).

`sheriff types` generates the following:

- from the RecordTypes, the types and the RuntimeChecks of the system's code, where the technical appendix says they stand;
- the files each generator the project's own guidelines add prints.

A project's generator is run by the command the settings name for it, and is handed what `sheriff model` prints on its standard input. It prints as JSON each file's path, its content and the sids of the nodes the file is written from (§s-km4x4s26kv7pjze).

`sheriff types` writes a file only where the file is missing or holds, line endings aside, anything other than what it generates. It names each file it writes.

`sheriff types` names each refusal and writes no file where one of the following holds:

- the check of generated copies refuses the list of the files it generates, or a RecordType (§s-dbk2vyeg8z5jxna);
- a project's generator ends with an exit code other than zero.

`sheriff types` writes a file only once every node that file is generated from is approved. It names each node it waits for:

- the types and RuntimeChecks wait for every RecordType;
- a file a project's generator prints waits for the nodes it is written from and for the rule listing that generator.

